  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Three thousand tons of steel vanished off a landlocked river. In 1898, the brand-new steamship Huronian undocked from a shipyard on the River Thames.",
        "visual_query": "victorian london river thames dock",
        "visual_fallback": "industrial river fog"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was destined for an open-water voyage, but never made it past the estuary. Port authorities watched the horizon, yet the towering freighter simply ceased to exist.",
        "visual_query": "foggy nineteenth century shipping harbor",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage cargo ship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "How does a vessel longer than a football field evaporate without leaving a single wooden crate or oil slick behind in the busy channel?",
        "visual_query": "choppy gray ocean waves",
        "visual_fallback": "stormy sea surface"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The secret lay in the dead of night. The iron ore cargo shifted during a violent winter gale off the British coast, tearing the hull apart instantly.",
        "visual_query": "dramatic stormy ocean waves",
        "visual_fallback": "dark stormy sea"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It hadn't vanished in the city at all; wreckage was later identified near the Welsh coast, confirming a catastrophic open-sea sinking.",
        "visual_query": "welsh rocky coastline waves",
        "visual_fallback": "coastal rocks surf"
      }
    ],
